# name: test/sql/core_alone.test
# description: M2 stage 3 -- every core macro runs with no tree-sitter language compiled in
# group: [sitting_duck]
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR
# ---------------------
# M2 stage 3 split src/sql_macros into a `core` half and an `addon` half, and
# gave nine core macros a `_from` variant that reads an AST out of a NAMED TABLE
# instead of parsing a path. The point of all of it is that the core half is
# language-agnostic: it should work in a build that has no tree-sitter parser at
# all, which is the duckling case (RFC:241, Teague's case 3).
#
# scripts/verify_macro_boundary.py asserts that STATICALLY, over macro text.
# This file asserts it LIVE: scripts/test_core_only_build.sh builds the
# extension with -DSITTING_DUCK_LANGUAGES=duckdb -- one native, non-tree-sitter
# adapter and zero tree-sitter parsers -- and runs this file against it.
#
# EVERY ASSERTION HERE MUST HOLD IN BOTH BUILDS. The file is run twice: against
# a full 28-language build (which proves it is not vacuous) and against the
# duckdb-only build (which is the claim). Anything whose answer DEPENDS on the
# language set therefore lives in scripts/test_core_only_build.sh, not here --
# the compiled-language list, and the macros that cannot bind without a
# tree-sitter parser. A build-dependent assertion in this file would have to be
# wrong in one of the two runs.
#
# WHY A COMMITTED FIXTURE AND NOT A LIVE PARSE. The only language in the
# restricted build is `duckdb`, and it cannot exercise much of core:
# src/unparse_rules.cpp has no `duckdb` rule-map entry, every node reports
# start_byte = end_byte = 0, every node reports start_line = end_line = 1, and
# every node reports an EMPTY file_path. A test that accepted the 0 rows that
# follow would be the vacuous gate ~/.CLAUDE.md forbids. So the AST comes from
# test/data/fixtures/core_alone_ast.csv: a python parse, committed, loaded into a
# build that cannot parse python. That is what the `_from` variants exist for.
#
# THE 69 CORE MACROS ARE ALL CALLED HERE, except one. The surface is DERIVED,
# not transcribed: scripts/test_core_only_build.sh re-derives the names with
# scripts/verify_macro_boundary.py's own parse_macros() over the `sql_macros`
# manifest in spec/layout/layout.yaml, and FAILS if any derived name has no
# call site in this file. A core macro added without a line here is therefore a
# red build, not a silent omission. Measured at this commit: 69 core macros of
# 105, 34 scalar and 35 table.
#
# (The brief for this task said 32 scalar / 37 table and "the 31 is_*
# predicates". Measured from duckdb_functions(): 34 scalar / 35 table, and there
# are 30 is_* predicates. The other four scalars are ast_qualified_name_as_string
# and the three ast_get_source* macros in file_utilities.sql, which are scalar
# subquery macros, not table macros.)
#
# THE ONE EXEMPTION, AND WHY IT IS AN EXEMPTION. See section 10.
#
# EVERY PINNED NUMBER AND DIGEST WAS MEASURED, not predicted. All of them come
# from build/release/duckdb (full 28-language build) at commit ca0b7fd, against
# this fixture. The fixture is static, so every count here is deterministic: the
# pins are "same answer" checks, not "non-empty" checks, which is what lets the
# restricted run mean something. A pin that moves is a finding to investigate,
# never a value to update -- with the two stated exceptions in sections 5b and 9,
# which pin KNOWN DEFECTS and are MEANT to move when those are fixed.

require sitting_duck

# =============================================================================
# 0. LOAD THE FIXTURE
# =============================================================================
#
# Three steps, and each one is load-bearing:
#
#   __fx_raw  reads every column as VARCHAR. `allow_quoted_nulls = false` is
#             NOT optional: DuckDB's CSV writer distinguishes an empty string
#             ("") from a NULL (an empty unquoted field), but the reader folds
#             `""` to NULL by default. MEASURED: with the default, 697 of 1246
#             rows came back with a NULL `name` where read_ast had an empty
#             string. header/delim/quote/escape are given explicitly because the
#             42-line `#` provenance block at the top of the file changes what
#             the sniffer sees.
#
#   __fx_u    casts to read_ast's exact types, with the three SEMANTIC_TYPE
#             sites still held as their UTINYINT codes.
#
#   core_ast  restores SEMANTIC_TYPE at all three sites, so the table is
#             read_ast's output in read_ast's types.
#
# WHY THE CODES AND NOT THE NAMES: SEMANTIC_TYPE -> VARCHAR IS LOSSY, AND THAT
# IS A FINDING IN ITS OWN RIGHT. MEASURED on this build: of the 256 codes, 192
# do not survive a round trip through their own printed name. Every name covers
# a BLOCK OF FOUR consecutive codes -- the low two bits are a refinement the
# name does not show -- and parsing the name returns the lowest code of the
# block:
#
#     244, 245, 246, 247 all print DEFINITION_VARIABLE
#     'DEFINITION_VARIABLE' parses back to 244
#
# On this file, writing names instead of codes silently re-typed 15 top-level
# values (codes 246, 149, 77, 78 and 154), 5 values inside qualified_name and 5
# inside scope.stack. The codes are exact.
#
# THAT ALSO LIMITS THIS BRANCH'S md5(r::VARCHAR) DIGEST CONVENTION, which the
# three macro_from_parity_*.test files and section 0b below all use. A whole-row
# cast to VARCHAR renders semantic_type by NAME, so it CANNOT SEE a change in a
# semantic type's low two bits -- the same blind spot the convention already
# documents for a widened numeric type. Worth knowing before trusting one of
# those digests about a semantic_type change.
#
# The cost of the code encoding is that this fixture is COUPLED TO THE ENUM'S
# NUMBERING: renumbering SEMANTIC_TYPE would re-type every node here without
# changing a byte of the CSV. The whole-row digest in section 0b is what catches
# that, and it is why the digest is here rather than only a row count.

statement ok
CREATE TABLE __fx_raw AS SELECT * FROM read_csv(
    'test/data/fixtures/core_alone_ast.csv',
    all_varchar = true, allow_quoted_nulls = false, comment = '#',
    header = true, delim = ',', quote = '"', escape = '"');

statement ok
CREATE TABLE __fx_u AS SELECT
  node_id::BIGINT AS node_id, type, semantic_type::UTINYINT AS st_u,
  flags::UTINYINT AS flags, name,
  qualified_name::STRUCT(semantic_type UTINYINT, "name" VARCHAR, "index" INTEGER)[] AS qn_u,
  signature_type,
  parameters::STRUCT("name" VARCHAR, "type" VARCHAR)[] AS parameters,
  modifiers::VARCHAR[] AS modifiers, annotations, receiver, file_path, language,
  start_line::UINTEGER AS start_line, end_line::UINTEGER AS end_line,
  parent_id::BIGINT AS parent_id, depth::UINTEGER AS depth,
  sibling_index::INTEGER AS sibling_index, children_count::UINTEGER AS children_count,
  descendant_count::UINTEGER AS descendant_count,
  scope::STRUCT("current" BIGINT, "function" BIGINT, "class" BIGINT, module BIGINT,
                stack STRUCT(id BIGINT, kind UTINYINT)[]) AS scope_u,
  peek, start_column::UINTEGER AS start_column, end_column::UINTEGER AS end_column,
  start_byte::UINTEGER AS start_byte, end_byte::UINTEGER AS end_byte
FROM __fx_raw;

statement ok
CREATE TABLE core_ast AS SELECT
  node_id, type, st_u::SEMANTIC_TYPE AS semantic_type, flags, name,
  list_transform(qn_u, lambda s: {'semantic_type': s.semantic_type::SEMANTIC_TYPE,
                                  'name': s.name, 'index': s.index}) AS qualified_name,
  signature_type, parameters, modifiers, annotations, receiver, file_path, language,
  start_line, end_line, parent_id, depth, sibling_index, children_count, descendant_count,
  {'current': scope_u.current, 'function': scope_u.function, 'class': scope_u.class,
   'module': scope_u.module,
   'stack': list_transform(scope_u.stack,
                lambda x: {'id': x.id, 'kind': x.kind::SEMANTIC_TYPE})} AS scope,
  peek, start_column, end_column, start_byte, end_byte
FROM __fx_u;

# =============================================================================
# 0b. THE FLOOR, AND THE FIXTURE'S IDENTITY
# =============================================================================
#
# FIRST, so that it is the floor under everything below. If the fixture failed
# to load, or loaded short, every count further down would be a smaller number
# rather than an error -- which is how a whole file of assertions becomes
# meaningless at once.
#
# 26 columns, not 22. read_ast's default projection has 22, and this fixture
# carries four more -- start_column, end_column, start_byte, end_byte -- joined
# from a `source := 'full'` parse of the same file. ast_unparse_exact_from and
# ast_unparse_exact_splice read start_byte/end_byte, so on a plain 22-column
# dump they do not return 0 rows, they fail to BIND: MEASURED, 'Binder Error:
# Values list "t" does not have a column named "start_byte"'. Two of the 69 core
# macros would have been uncallable. The fixture's header records that the
# generator measured 0 disagreements between the two parses on every shared
# column.
#
# THE DIGEST IS NOT DECORATION. It is what makes the 1246 mean "the same 1246
# nodes": a row count cannot see a re-typed semantic_type, and md5 over
# r::VARCHAR prints every column name and value, so a dropped, added or renamed
# column moves it. It does NOT see every retype -- MEASURED,
# `{'x': 5::INTEGER}::VARCHAR = {'x': 5::BIGINT}::VARCHAR` is true -- so a
# widened numeric type is invisible here. The ORDER BY is node_id, which is
# unique in this table; without it string_agg's order is unspecified and the
# digest would be nondeterministic.
query II
SELECT count(*), (SELECT count(*) FROM (DESCRIBE SELECT * FROM core_ast)) FROM core_ast;
----
1246	26

query I
SELECT md5(string_agg(r::VARCHAR, ',' ORDER BY r.node_id)) FROM core_ast r;
----
68975e9e028fdbe48953bcd02bfccb1a

# =============================================================================
# 1. file_utilities.sql -- the three SCALAR source readers (3 of 69 core macros called)
# =============================================================================
#
# ast_get_source, ast_get_source_numbered and ast_get_source_line take a PATH
# and read it with read_text(). They need no parser at all, so they are called
# against the fixture's own source file, which is committed beside it. Lines
# 12-14 are the `__init__` whose node is node_id 42 in the fixture, so the
# arguments come from the AST rather than from a guess.
#
# Length AND digest, not digest alone: an md5 that moved would say "different"
# and nothing else, and the length says in which direction.
query III
SELECT length(ast_get_source('test/data/python/sample_app.py', 12, 14)),
       md5(ast_get_source('test/data/python/sample_app.py', 12, 14)),
       md5(ast_get_source_numbered('test/data/python/sample_app.py', 12, 14));
----
77	fe1443c8b67b6f5501806c27e9f6b9d5	face93234ee229f3216cf961aeed0452

query I
SELECT md5(ast_get_source_line('test/data/python/sample_app.py', 1));
----
ad0644f76feee9ad14c5de4d74cc0cac

# =============================================================================
# 2. semantic_predicates.sql -- all 30 is_* predicates (33 of 69)
# =============================================================================
#
# A row count is meaningless for a scalar predicate, so each one is measured
# twice, in the same row:
#
#   dom_hits  how many of the 256 possible SEMANTIC_TYPE values satisfy it.
#             This is the predicate's own domain and has nothing to do with the
#             fixture. A predicate with dom_hits = 0 can NEVER be true for any
#             input, which is a defect in the predicate, not a gap in the
#             fixture -- see the note below.
#
#             WHY NEARLY EVERY dom_hits IS 4: each semantic type NAME covers a
#             block of four consecutive codes, as section 0 records. is_literal
#             reads 16 because it matches on the 'LITERAL' prefix and so spans
#             four such names.
#
#   fix_hits  how many of the fixture's 1246 nodes satisfy it.
#
# PAIRING THEM IS THE POINT, and it is ruling R34's shape. A fix_hits of 0 on
# its own is ambiguous: it could mean "python has no such node" or "the
# predicate is broken" or "the fixture did not load". With dom_hits beside it,
# and with 1246 already pinned above as the floor, a 0 in fix_hits is a
# statement about python and a 0 in dom_hits is a statement about the enum. A
# wholly broken load cannot produce this table, because 21 of the 30 fix_hits
# are non-zero -- 9 are 0 -- and the 21 would all go to 0 together.
#
# TWO PREDICATES ARE TRUE FOR NO VALUE AT ALL, and that is a reported defect,
# not an exemption:
#
#   is_boolean_literal  is_semantic_type(st, 'LITERAL_BOOLEAN')
#   is_type_definition  is_semantic_type(st, 'DEFINITION_TYPE')
#
# Neither name exists in the SEMANTIC_TYPE enum -- MEASURED, both cast from
# VARCHAR to SEMANTIC_TYPE as NULL, and both predicates return false for all 256
# values. So these two macros are dead as written. They are pinned here at 0/0
# so the defect has a witness; fixing them is a behaviour change and therefore
# outside M2 stage 3, which is constrained to no behaviour change.
#
# This is NOT the same thing as the lossy name round trip in section 0. There,
# real names map to a block of four codes and lose the low two bits. Here the
# names do not exist at all.
#
# THE SEVEN PREDICATES with dom_hits = 4 and fix_hits = 0 are a different and
# benign case: they work, and python's grammar simply has no node of that
# semantic type. They are is_export, is_foreign, is_directive, is_type_primitive,
# is_type_composite, is_type_reference and is_type_generic -- three plus the four
# type-* families, which is seven, and the two dead ones above bring the column
# to nine zeros.
#
# Widening the fixture cannot fix those seven: no single python file has a node
# that is semantically an export or an FFI declaration, and the only other
# language in the restricted build is `duckdb`, whose adapter has none of them
# either -- so a second fixture would have to come from a THIRD language's
# committed parse. They are CALLED, and their domain behaviour is asserted in
# the same row, which is the assertion that has power here.
query III
WITH dom AS (SELECT i::UTINYINT::SEMANTIC_TYPE AS st FROM range(0,256) t(i)),
m(pred, dom_hits, fix_hits) AS (
  SELECT 'is_annotation', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_annotation(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_annotation(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_arithmetic', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_arithmetic(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_arithmetic(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_assignment', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_assignment(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_assignment(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_block', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_block(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_block(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_boolean_literal', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_boolean_literal(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_boolean_literal(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_class_definition', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_class_definition(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_class_definition(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_comment', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_comment(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_comment(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_comparison', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_comparison(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_comparison(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_conditional', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_conditional(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_conditional(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_directive', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_directive(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_directive(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_export', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_export(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_export(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_foreign', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_foreign(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_foreign(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_function_call', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_function_call(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_function_call(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_function_definition', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_function_definition(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_function_definition(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_import', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_import(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_import(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_jump', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_jump(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_jump(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_list', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_list(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_list(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_literal', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_literal(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_literal(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_logical', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_logical(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_logical(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_loop', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_loop(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_loop(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_member_access', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_member_access(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_member_access(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_module_definition', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_module_definition(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_module_definition(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_number_literal', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_number_literal(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_number_literal(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_string_literal', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_string_literal(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_string_literal(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_type_composite', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_type_composite(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_type_composite(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_type_definition', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_type_definition(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_type_definition(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_type_generic', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_type_generic(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_type_generic(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_type_primitive', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_type_primitive(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_type_primitive(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_type_reference', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_type_reference(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_type_reference(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast) UNION ALL
  SELECT 'is_variable_definition', (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_variable_definition(st)) FROM dom), (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_variable_definition(semantic_type)) FROM core_ast)
)
SELECT pred, dom_hits, fix_hits FROM m ORDER BY pred;
----
is_annotation	4	4
is_arithmetic	4	6
is_assignment	4	32
is_block	4	52
is_boolean_literal	0	0
is_class_definition	4	6
is_comment	4	2
is_comparison	4	19
is_conditional	4	28
is_directive	4	0
is_export	4	0
is_foreign	4	0
is_function_call	4	45
is_function_definition	4	40
is_import	4	7
is_jump	4	38
is_list	4	66
is_literal	16	96
is_logical	4	6
is_loop	4	10
is_member_access	4	56
is_module_definition	4	1
is_number_literal	4	9
is_string_literal	4	72
is_type_composite	4	0
is_type_definition	0	0
is_type_generic	4	0
is_type_primitive	4	0
is_type_reference	4	0
is_variable_definition	4	31

# ast_qualified_name_as_string -- the 31st macro in semantic_predicates.sql, and
# the one scalar there that is not a predicate (34 of 69).
#
# It is the one macro in this file that reads a NESTED SEMANTIC_TYPE: its body
# passes s.semantic_type from each qualified_name segment to
# is_function_definition / is_class_definition / is_variable_definition /
# is_module_definition. So this assertion is also the check that section 0's
# nested cast worked. MEASURED on the full build: the same digest comes back
# from a live read_ast() parse as from this fixture.
query II
SELECT count(*) FILTER (ast_qualified_name_as_string(qualified_name) IS NOT NULL),
       md5(string_agg(ast_qualified_name_as_string(qualified_name), '|' ORDER BY node_id))
FROM core_ast;
----
45	0aac32b07dbaeddb366a5854df6a486f

# =============================================================================
# 3. tree_navigation.sql -- the 11 table-taking macros (45 of 69)
# =============================================================================
#
# Every argument is an anchor taken from the fixture, not invented:
#   node_id 0   the module root, children_count 16, descendant_count 1245
#   node_id 32  class_definition `Config`, lines 10-22, descendant_count 117
#   node_id 42  function_definition `__init__`, lines 12-14
#   node_id 71  function_definition `load`, lines 16-19
#   node_id 85  call `open`, line 17
#
# ast_definitions_from and ast_definition_parent both return 42 here, and that
# is not a copy-paste. MEASURED: 42 is the number of nodes that pass
# ast_definitions_from's FULL filter chain -- is_definition(semantic_type) AND
# is_construct(flags) AND name <> '' -- and ast_definition_parent emits one row
# per such node. The chain matters: this file has 78 nodes with a definition
# semantic type, and 55 of those are construct-flagged, so neither of those two
# figures is the answer. The macro's own banner says why (a keyword token shares
# the semantic type of the construct it introduces, so is_definition alone
# double-counts).
#
# ONE ROW PER MACRO, so a failure names the macro. A single wide row of 11
# counts would have the same assertion count and tell you only that something
# moved.
query II
SELECT 'ast_ancestors' AS macro, count(*) AS n FROM ast_ancestors('core_ast', 85) UNION ALL
SELECT 'ast_call_arguments', count(*) FROM ast_call_arguments('core_ast', 85) UNION ALL
SELECT 'ast_children', count(*) FROM ast_children('core_ast', 0) UNION ALL
SELECT 'ast_class_members', count(*) FROM ast_class_members('core_ast', 32) UNION ALL
SELECT 'ast_containing_line_from', count(*) FROM ast_containing_line_from('core_ast', 17) UNION ALL
SELECT 'ast_definition_parent', count(*) FROM ast_definition_parent('core_ast') UNION ALL
SELECT 'ast_definitions_from', count(*) FROM ast_definitions_from('core_ast') UNION ALL
SELECT 'ast_descendants', count(*) FROM ast_descendants('core_ast', 32) UNION ALL
SELECT 'ast_function_scope', count(*) FROM ast_function_scope('core_ast', 71) UNION ALL
SELECT 'ast_in_range_from', count(*) FROM ast_in_range_from('core_ast', 10, 22) UNION ALL
SELECT 'ast_siblings', count(*) FROM ast_siblings('core_ast', 42)
ORDER BY 1;
----
ast_ancestors	10
ast_call_arguments	1
ast_children	16
ast_class_members	3
ast_containing_line_from	23
ast_definition_parent	42
ast_definitions_from	42
ast_descendants	117
ast_function_scope	46
ast_in_range_from	118
ast_siblings	3

# =============================================================================
# 4. relational_operators.sql -- the five `_from` variants (50 of 69)
# =============================================================================
#
# ast_has_from and ast_not_has_from are a complementary pair over the same two
# types, so 17 and 3 are not two independent numbers. MEASURED: this file has 20
# nodes of type function_definition and all 20 are construct-flagged; 17 of them
# contain a call and 3 do not. If a defect dropped rows from the shared body,
# one of the two would fall and the partition would stop adding up. The next
# block is that control, with the 20 measured INDEPENDENTLY from core_ast rather
# than as the sum of the two calls being checked.
query II
SELECT 'ast_follows_from' AS macro, count(*) AS n FROM ast_follows_from('core_ast','function_definition','class_definition') UNION ALL
SELECT 'ast_has_from', count(*) FROM ast_has_from('core_ast','function_definition','call') UNION ALL
SELECT 'ast_inside_from', count(*) FROM ast_inside_from('core_ast','call','function_definition') UNION ALL
SELECT 'ast_not_has_from', count(*) FROM ast_not_has_from('core_ast','function_definition','call') UNION ALL
SELECT 'ast_precedes_from', count(*) FROM ast_precedes_from('core_ast','class_definition','function_definition')
ORDER BY 1;
----
ast_follows_from	5
ast_has_from	17
ast_inside_from	44
ast_not_has_from	3
ast_precedes_from	3

# THE PARTITION, with an INDEPENDENT denominator. The third column counts
# function_definition nodes straight out of core_ast, so it is not the sum of
# the first two and the row has real power: if the shared `_from` body dropped
# or duplicated rows, columns 1 and 2 would stop partitioning column 3. An
# earlier draft of this row made the third column `col1 + col2`, which is
# arithmetic rather than a measurement and could never disagree with itself.
query III
SELECT (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_has_from('core_ast','function_definition','call')),
       (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_not_has_from('core_ast','function_definition','call')),
       (SELECT count(*) FILTER (type = 'function_definition' AND is_construct(flags)) FROM core_ast);
----
17	3	20

# =============================================================================
# 5. file_utilities.sql -- ast_source_of_from (51 of 69)
# =============================================================================
#
# The only `_from` variant that keeps a second argument: its result carries the
# source TEXT of each match, which comes from read_text(files), and a node table
# holds no text.
#
# tracker/bugs/058 IS A HAZARD ON THIS LINE. The macro inner-joins
# read_text(files).filename against the table's file_path on exact string
# equality (src/sql_macros/file_utilities.sql:164), so a path spelled two ways
# returns 0 rows and raises nothing. The fixture's file_path column holds
# 'test/data/python/sample_app.py', with no './', because that is the spelling
# read_ast was given when the fixture was generated -- so that is the spelling
# passed here. This is not a globber's doing; the CSV carries whatever the
# generator wrote.
query II
SELECT count(*), md5(string_agg(source, '|' ORDER BY file_path, start_line))
FROM ast_source_of_from('core_ast', 'test/data/python/sample_app.py', 'load');
----
1	9e3a947549183f681562bcfe584f9926

# 5b. THE 058 TRAP, PINNED -- A KNOWN DEFECT, AND THESE TWO NUMBERS ARE MEANT
# TO MOVE WHEN IT IS FIXED.
#
# Same table, same target, two spellings of the same file. The agreeing spelling
# returns 1 and is the positive control; the './'-prefixed one returns 0 with no
# error. The 1 sits in the same row as the 0 on purpose: a 0 beside a 1 is a
# statement about the spelling, and a wholly broken query cannot produce it.
#
# tracker/bugs/058 says what this becomes when it is fixed: either 1 and 1 (if
# the normalising join lands alone) or a `statement error` naming the unmatched
# file (if the readability guard raises first). The file-header rule that a pin
# which moves is a failure to investigate does NOT apply to the second column
# here, and this comment is the only place that exception is granted.
query II
SELECT (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_source_of_from('core_ast','test/data/python/sample_app.py','load')),
       (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_source_of_from('core_ast','./test/data/python/sample_app.py','load'));
----
1	0

# =============================================================================
# 6. ast_unparse.sql -- the rules, and the five callable unparse macros
# =============================================================================
#
# THE FACT THIS WHOLE FIXTURE APPROACH RESTS ON, confirmed at runtime rather
# than inferred from the source. src/unparse_rules.cpp #includes all 53
# languages' *_unparse.def files UNCONDITIONALLY, so python's unparse rules are
# compiled in even when no python PARSER is. Reading that in the source is not
# the same claim as measuring it in the binary, so this is the measurement:
# ast_unparse_rules('python') carries 8 rows tagged 'python' on top of the 15
# universal ones. In a build with no tree-sitter language at all, those 8 rows
# are rules for a language the binary cannot parse -- which is exactly the
# separation this task exists to demonstrate.
query III
SELECT count(*), count(*) FILTER (language = '*'), count(*) FILTER (language = 'python')
FROM ast_unparse_rules('python');
----
23	15	8

# THE REVIEW FOCUS CASE THIS TASK OWNS, part 1: the real shape of "a language
# with no rules". `duckdb` has no entry in the 53-entry rule map, so
# ast_unparse_rules('duckdb') returns the universal rules and nothing else.
#
# THE COUNT IS 15, NOT 6. The task brief said "exactly the 6 universal rules".
# MEASURED on build/release/duckdb at ca0b7fd: 15 rows, all tagged '*', 0 rows
# tagged with any language. They are the TIGHT_BEFORE/TIGHT_AFTER punctuation
# rules for , ; : ) ] } ( [ { . -> :: -- 15 of them, not 6. The "all universal,
# no language rows" half of the brief's claim holds; the number did not.
query III
SELECT count(*), count(*) FILTER (language = '*'), count(*) FILTER (language <> '*')
FROM ast_unparse_rules('duckdb');
----
15	15	0

# ast_unparse_from (52 of 69) and ast_unparse_custom (53 of 69).
#
# They return the SAME digest, and that is a measured fact rather than a
# coincidence worth hiding: ast_unparse_custom is handed ast_unparse_rules
# ('python') itself, which is what ast_unparse_from resolves internally for a
# python AST. Passing a DIFFERENT rules table is what would separate them, and
# that belongs to ast_unparse's own tests, not to this one. What this asserts is
# that both bodies run and agree in a parser-free build.
#
# This digest is the single most useful pin in the file for the restricted run:
# it is built from the COMPILED unparse rules, so it is the output most likely to
# differ if the restricted build lost them.
statement ok
CREATE TABLE py_rules AS SELECT * FROM ast_unparse_rules('python');

query III
SELECT 'ast_unparse_from' AS macro, count(*) AS n, md5(string_agg(source,'|' ORDER BY file_path)) AS d FROM ast_unparse_from('core_ast') UNION ALL
SELECT 'ast_unparse_custom', count(*), md5(string_agg(source,'|' ORDER BY file_path)) FROM ast_unparse_custom('core_ast','py_rules')
ORDER BY 1;
----
ast_unparse_custom	1	f56867bdb1fefc8835a13034bef15a33
ast_unparse_from	1	f56867bdb1fefc8835a13034bef15a33

# ast_unparse_code (54 of 69). Its body is a third independent copy of the
# unparse logic -- ast_unparse_from, ast_unparse and ast_unparse_code each carry
# their own ~100 lines -- so calling it is not covered by the two above. It takes
# the language as an argument, so it can be driven through `duckdb`, which is the
# one language present in both builds.
query II
SELECT count(*), md5(string_agg(source,'|'))
FROM ast_unparse_code('SELECT a, b FROM t WHERE a = 1','duckdb');
----
1	5324034c8399fdeabcd8973cdead1ac1

# ast_unparse_exact_splice (55 of 69) and ast_unparse_exact_from (56 of 69).
#
# THIS IS THE STRONGEST NON-VACUITY EVIDENCE IN THE FILE. The byte-exact law is
# `unparse(parse(f)) == f`. Here the parse is a CSV committed to git and the
# build cannot parse python at all, yet the macro reassembles the file byte for
# byte from the fixture's start_byte/end_byte and the file's own bytes. A
# fixture that had lost or reordered a single node would fail this, because
# ast_unparse_exact_splice also checks that the node table is a complete tree
# (n_rows = root descendant_count + 1) and that the leaf frontier is an
# antichain. `bool_and` over the single row rather than a bare `=` so that a
# zero-row result reads as NULL, not as a pass.
#
# ast_unparse_exact_splice takes the bytes as a TABLE of (fp, cblob), which is
# the shape ast_unparse_exact_from builds internally from read_blob(files). Both
# forms are called, so both the splice body and the wrapper are exercised.
statement ok
CREATE TABLE src_blob AS
    SELECT filename AS fp, content AS cblob FROM read_blob('test/data/python/sample_app.py');

query III
SELECT 'ast_unparse_exact_from' AS macro, count(*) AS n,
       bool_and(source = (SELECT content FROM read_text('test/data/python/sample_app.py'))) AS byte_exact
FROM ast_unparse_exact_from('core_ast','test/data/python/sample_app.py') UNION ALL
SELECT 'ast_unparse_exact_splice', count(*),
       bool_and(source = (SELECT content FROM read_text('test/data/python/sample_app.py')))
FROM ast_unparse_exact_splice('core_ast','src_blob')
ORDER BY 1;
----
ast_unparse_exact_from	1	true
ast_unparse_exact_splice	1	true

# ast_unparse_exact (57 of 69) and ast_unparse_exact_code (58 of 69).
#
# These two take a PATH and a CODE STRING, so in a parser-free build the only
# language they can be driven through is `duckdb` -- whose adapter reports
# start_byte = end_byte = 0 for every node. The macro's own substrate guard
# catches that and names it. So these are CALLED, and what is asserted is the
# named error, which is a positive assertion and not an absence: a macro that
# failed to bind, or that silently returned text built from nothing, would not
# produce this message.
#
# MEASURED build-invariant: the message comes from the macro body and the duckdb
# adapter, neither of which depends on which other languages are compiled in.
statement error
SELECT * FROM ast_unparse_exact('test/data/sql/example.sql','duckdb');
----
every node reports start_byte = end_byte = 0

statement error
SELECT * FROM ast_unparse_exact_code('SELECT a, b FROM t WHERE a = 1','duckdb');
----
every node reports start_byte = end_byte = 0

# =============================================================================
# 7. The eight PATH-TAKING twins, driven through `duckdb` (66 of 69)
# =============================================================================
#
# These macros call read_ast() themselves, so a fixture cannot reach them. They
# are reachable anyway, and that was worth measuring rather than assuming: each
# one takes `language :=`, and the `duckdb` adapter is NATIVE -- it wraps
# DuckDB's own parser instead of a tree-sitter grammar, so it is present in a
# build with zero tree-sitter languages. Pointing them at a committed .sql file
# with language := 'duckdb' therefore exercises the wrapper body that the `_from`
# form does not contain.
#
# THE NUMBERS ARE SMALL AND TWO OF THEM ARE DEGENERATE, so read them with the
# adapter in mind rather than as a quality signal. MEASURED on
# test/data/sql/example.sql (20 nodes): the duckdb adapter reports
# start_line = end_line = 1 for EVERY node, which is why ast_containing_line(1)
# and ast_in_range(1,12) both return all 20 rows and ast_containing_line(3)
# returns none. What these assertions establish is that each wrapper BINDS and
# RUNS without a tree-sitter parser, which is this task's claim; the richness of
# the result is the adapter's business.
query II
SELECT 'ast_containing_line' AS macro, count(*) AS n FROM ast_containing_line('test/data/sql/example.sql', 1, language := 'duckdb') UNION ALL
SELECT 'ast_definitions', count(*) FROM ast_definitions('test/data/sql/example.sql', language := 'duckdb') UNION ALL
SELECT 'ast_follows', count(*) FROM ast_follows('test/data/sql/example.sql','where_clause','select_list', language := 'duckdb') UNION ALL
SELECT 'ast_has', count(*) FROM ast_has('test/data/sql/example.sql','select_node','column_reference', language := 'duckdb') UNION ALL
SELECT 'ast_in_range', count(*) FROM ast_in_range('test/data/sql/example.sql', 1, 12, language := 'duckdb') UNION ALL
SELECT 'ast_inside', count(*) FROM ast_inside('test/data/sql/example.sql','column_reference','where_clause', language := 'duckdb') UNION ALL
SELECT 'ast_not_has', count(*) FROM ast_not_has('test/data/sql/example.sql','select_node','insert_statement', language := 'duckdb') UNION ALL
SELECT 'ast_precedes', count(*) FROM ast_precedes('test/data/sql/example.sql','select_list','where_clause', language := 'duckdb')
ORDER BY 1;
----
ast_containing_line	20
ast_definitions	1
ast_follows	1
ast_has	1
ast_in_range	20
ast_inside	1
ast_not_has	1
ast_precedes	1

# ast_source_of (67 of 69) -- CALLED, and its 0 is EXPLAINED IN THE SAME ROW.
#
# This is the ninth path-taking twin and the one that cannot return rows through
# `duckdb`, for a reason that belongs to the ADAPTER and not to the macro:
# MEASURED, all 20 nodes of a duckdb parse carry an EMPTY file_path. The macro
# joins read_text(files).filename against that file_path on exact equality, so
# the join matches nothing. That is tracker/bugs/058's silent-empty path reached
# through the adapter rather than through a './' prefix -- a second instance of
# 058, and reported as one.
#
# Four columns, so the 0 has three controls beside it and cannot be produced by
# a broken query: the definition the macro is looking for IS there (control_defs
# = 1, the `customers` table), the flag filter the macro applies DOES pass
# (construct_flagged = 20), and the cause is named (blank_paths = 20). The
# macro's body is ast_source_of_from's, which section 5 exercises on the fixture
# and gets a row from.
#
# THIS IS NOT THE FILE'S EXEMPTION. The exemption is section 10.
query IIII
SELECT (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_definitions('test/data/sql/example.sql', language := 'duckdb')) AS control_defs,
       (SELECT count(*) FILTER (is_construct(flags)) FROM read_ast('test/data/sql/example.sql','duckdb')) AS construct_flagged,
       (SELECT count(*) FILTER (file_path = '' OR file_path IS NULL) FROM read_ast('test/data/sql/example.sql','duckdb')) AS blank_paths,
       (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_source_of('test/data/sql/example.sql','customers', language := 'duckdb')) AS source_of_rows;
----
1	20	20	0

# =============================================================================
# 8. parse_ast_list_table.sql -- the one macro in its file (68 of 69)
# =============================================================================
#
# Takes code and a language rather than a table, so it is driven through
# `duckdb` like section 7. Its body projects n.qualified_name and n.scope, so a
# bind failure on the nested STRUCT types would show up here.
query I
SELECT count(*) FROM parse_ast_list_table('SELECT a, b FROM t WHERE a = 1','duckdb');
----
11

# =============================================================================
# 9. THE REVIEW FOCUS CASE, part 2 -- A KNOWN DEFECT, PINNED, NOT FIXED
# =============================================================================
#
# Section 6 established that ast_unparse_rules('duckdb') returns the 15
# universal rules and no language rules. The question the Review Focus line was
# written to catch is what the rules-based unparse path then DOES with a duckdb
# AST. The brief said to measure it and not to assume either answer.
#
# MEASURED: it does not raise. It returns one row of text built from the 15
# universal rules, and the text is wrong -- every keyword, punctuation mark and
# operator is gone, because the duckdb adapter's tree is an AST and not a CST
# and so has no such nodes. `SELECT a, b FROM t WHERE a = 1` comes back as
# `a b t a 1`.
#
# THAT IS THE HAZARD. A caller gets plausible-looking text instead of an error,
# which is the silent-wrong-answer class that ast_unparse_exact refuses by
# design (its guard is asserted in section 6). WHAT IT SHOULD BECOME: the same
# treatment as ast_unparse_exact -- a named error saying that this adapter's
# tree cannot be unparsed, pointing at src/sql_macros/ast_unparse.sql:502-515,
# which already carries the "NOT SERVED: the `duckdb` language adapter" note.
#
# NOT FIXED HERE, deliberately. A change to the rules-based unparse path is
# outside this task and outside M2 stage 3's no-behaviour-change constraint.
# This pin is the witness, and it is MEANT to move -- to a `statement error` --
# when the fix lands. The file-header rule about pins that move does not apply
# to this block, and this comment is the only place that exception is granted.
statement ok
CREATE TABLE dd_ast AS SELECT * FROM parse_ast('SELECT a, b FROM t WHERE a = 1','duckdb');

query II
SELECT count(*), string_agg(source,'|') FROM ast_unparse_from('dd_ast');
----
1	a b t a 1

# =============================================================================
# 10. THE ONE EXEMPTION: ast_unparse(path, preset) (69 of 69)
# =============================================================================
#
# THE REASON IS A PROPERTY OF THE BUILD, not of the macro's output, and it is
# measured: `ast_supported_languages()` gives `duckdb` an EMPTY extensions list.
# It is the one language with no file extension of its own, because it is
# selected explicitly rather than detected. So in a build whose only language is
# duckdb, NO path argument resolves to a language, and ast_unparse -- alone among
# the path-taking core macros -- has no `language :=` parameter to say so with.
# Sections 7 and 8 reach every other path- or code-taking macro precisely because
# each of them does have one.
#
# "It returns 0 rows" is not the reason and is not true: it raises.
#
# WIDENING THE FIXTURE CANNOT HELP, which is the test ruling 2 asks for. The
# fixture is a TABLE and this macro takes a PATH; no fixture is reachable from
# it. Adding a second language to the build would make it callable and would
# also delete the thing this task proves.
#
# WHAT IS STILL ASSERTED. The macro is CALLED, and the named error is pinned --
# which is a positive assertion. It establishes that ast_unparse binds, that its
# body reaches read_ast, and that a path with no resolvable language fails loudly
# rather than returning empty. The .csv extension belongs to no language in
# EITHER build, so this assertion is build-invariant, which is why it can live
# here. What is NOT established is any result from ast_unparse's own ~100-line
# body; that body is a third copy of the unparse logic, and the other two copies
# are exercised in section 6.
statement error
SELECT * FROM ast_unparse('test/data/fixtures/core_alone_ast.csv');
----
Could not detect language

# =============================================================================
# 11. WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT PROVE
# =============================================================================
#
# Stated here because the gap is easy to misread as covered.
#
# IT DOES NOT PROVE THE CORE RUNS WITHOUT THE ADD-ON MACROS. The language
# selection and the SQL macro set are independent: cmake regenerates
# src/include/embedded_sql_macros.hpp from the whole `sql_macros` manifest
# regardless of -DSITTING_DUCK_LANGUAGES, and the controller measured that the
# header comes back byte-identical under a restricted language set. So all 105
# macros -- both sides of the boundary -- are registered in the duckdb-only
# build too. scripts/test_core_only_build.sh re-measures that and prints it.
#
# What this file proves is the other half, and the half that has no other guard:
# that the core macros run with no TREE-SITTER LANGUAGE compiled in. A core
# macro that reached an add-on macro would still succeed here, because the
# add-on macro is still registered. The boundary itself stays the business of
# scripts/verify_macro_boundary.py and test/sql/macro_split_oracle.test.
